1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,520
Five point nine billion dollars.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:06,064
That is how much Costco collected in

3
00:00:06,064 --> 00:00:07,310
a single year,

4
00:00:07,310 --> 00:00:09,320
before anyone bought a thing,

5
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:11,101
just for the right to walk through

6
00:00:11,101 --> 00:00:12,209
its doors.

7
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:15,111
Now compare it with Costco's operating

8
00:00:15,111 --> 00:00:16,781
profit for the same year,

9
00:00:16,781 --> 00:00:19,800
eleven point seven billion dollars.

10
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:21,540
By our calculation,

11
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:23,923
the membership fees alone equal about half

12
00:00:23,923 --> 00:00:24,920
of it.

13
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,457
On the goods it sells,

14
00:00:26,457 --> 00:00:29,826
Costco barely makes any money at all.

15
00:00:30,083 --> 00:00:31,870
So how does a store that sells

16
00:00:31,870 --> 00:00:34,167
almost at cost become one of the

17
00:00:34,167 --> 00:00:37,829
most powerful retailers in America?

18
00:00:38,083 --> 00:00:39,996
By the end of this video,

19
00:00:39,996 --> 00:00:41,692
you will know the four parts of

20
00:00:41,692 --> 00:00:43,503
the Costco machine.

21
00:00:43,503 --> 00:00:44,807
The thin margin,

22
00:00:44,807 --> 00:00:46,376
the membership card,

23
00:00:46,376 --> 00:00:47,946
the short shopping list,

24
00:00:47,946 --> 00:00:50,606
and the money it uses before it

25
00:00:50,606 --> 00:00:52,270
pays its suppliers.

26
00:00:57,917 --> 00:01:00,607
Start with the size of it.

27
00:01:00,647 --> 00:01:03,112
In its financial year that ended in

28
00:01:03,112 --> 00:01:05,059
August twenty twenty-six,

29
00:01:05,059 --> 00:01:08,017
Costco sold two hundred and ninety-seven

30
00:01:08,017 --> 00:01:09,964
billion dollars worth of goods,

31
00:01:09,964 --> 00:01:11,936
up ten point one percent from two

32
00:01:11,936 --> 00:01:14,401
hundred and sixty-nine point nine billion

33
00:01:14,401 --> 00:01:15,987
the year before.

34
00:01:16,277 --> 00:01:18,122
It runs nine hundred and thirty-nine

35
00:01:18,122 --> 00:01:19,947
warehouses around the world,

36
00:01:19,947 --> 00:01:22,202
six hundred and forty-seven of them in

37
00:01:22,202 --> 00:01:25,057
the United States and Puerto Rico.

38
00:01:25,327 --> 00:01:26,159
By sales,

39
00:01:26,159 --> 00:01:28,292
it is one of the biggest retailers

40
00:01:28,292 --> 00:01:29,745
on the planet.

41
00:01:29,983 --> 00:01:32,193
But look at what it keeps.

42
00:01:32,253 --> 00:01:34,570
After paying for the goods themselves,

43
00:01:34,570 --> 00:01:37,145
Costco's gross margin was about eleven

44
00:01:37,145 --> 00:01:38,973
point one percent of sales.

45
00:01:38,973 --> 00:01:40,527
Put another way,

46
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:42,343
by our calculation,

47
00:01:42,343 --> 00:01:45,240
the average item was sold for only

48
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:47,610
about twelve and a half percent more

49
00:01:47,610 --> 00:01:50,053
than it cost Costco to buy.

50
00:01:50,723 --> 00:01:53,082
Costco itself says it operates at

51
00:01:53,082 --> 00:01:55,440
significantly lower margins than most

52
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:57,113
other retailers.

53
00:01:57,350 --> 00:01:59,305
And that thin slice still has to

54
00:01:59,305 --> 00:02:01,860
pay for everything else.

55
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:03,095
The warehouses,

56
00:02:03,095 --> 00:02:03,898
the workers,

57
00:02:03,898 --> 00:02:04,495
the trucks,

58
00:02:04,495 --> 00:02:06,330
the electricity.

59
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:07,940
After all of that,

60
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,461
by our calculation,

61
00:02:09,461 --> 00:02:11,886
Costco's operating profit was less than

62
00:02:11,886 --> 00:02:14,910
four cents on every dollar of revenue.

63
00:02:15,170 --> 00:02:17,325
Its net income for the year was

64
00:02:17,325 --> 00:02:19,740
nine point two billion dollars.

65
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:20,764
A big number,

66
00:02:20,764 --> 00:02:22,853
until you set it against nearly three

67
00:02:22,853 --> 00:02:25,333
hundred billion dollars of sales.

68
00:02:25,583 --> 00:02:27,821
Let me draw where one hundred dollars

69
00:02:27,821 --> 00:02:29,913
at the register goes.

70
00:02:29,963 --> 00:02:31,859
About eighty-nine dollars pays the

71
00:02:31,859 --> 00:02:33,483
supplier for the goods.

72
00:02:33,483 --> 00:02:35,643
About eleven dollars is left.

73
00:02:35,643 --> 00:02:37,512
Most of that pays for the stores

74
00:02:37,512 --> 00:02:38,813
and the staff.

75
00:02:38,813 --> 00:02:40,963
What remains is a tiny sliver of

76
00:02:40,963 --> 00:02:41,993
profit.

77
00:02:42,203 --> 00:02:43,854
Hold on to that picture,

78
00:02:43,854 --> 00:02:46,246
because the real profit is coming from

79
00:02:46,246 --> 00:02:47,803
somewhere else.

80
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:50,493
If the goods barely make money,

81
00:02:50,493 --> 00:02:52,820
where does the profit come from?

82
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:54,496
It starts at the door,

83
00:02:54,496 --> 00:02:56,770
with a small plastic card.

84
00:03:07,417 --> 00:03:08,800
To shop at Costco,

85
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:10,807
you need a membership.

86
00:03:10,817 --> 00:03:12,790
At the end of its last financial

87
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:13,207
year,

88
00:03:13,207 --> 00:03:15,618
Costco had about eighty-four million paid

89
00:03:15,618 --> 00:03:16,254
members,

90
00:03:16,254 --> 00:03:18,666
and about one hundred and fifty million

91
00:03:18,666 --> 00:03:21,297
cardholders once family and business cards

92
00:03:21,297 --> 00:03:22,555
are counted.

93
00:03:22,817 --> 00:03:25,276
In September twenty twenty-four,

94
00:03:25,276 --> 00:03:27,760
Costco raised its basic fee in the

95
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:30,988
United States and Canada from sixty to

96
00:03:30,988 --> 00:03:33,327
sixty-five dollars a year.

97
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:35,600
The Executive membership went from one

98
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:37,539
hundred and twenty to one hundred and

99
00:03:37,539 --> 00:03:38,527
thirty.

100
00:03:38,777 --> 00:03:40,844
The increase touched around fifty-two

101
00:03:40,844 --> 00:03:42,407
million memberships.

102
00:03:42,407 --> 00:03:44,857
Costco says the higher fee accounted for

103
00:03:44,857 --> 00:03:47,084
about thirty percent of the growth in

104
00:03:47,084 --> 00:03:49,089
its membership income in twenty

105
00:03:49,089 --> 00:03:50,357
twenty-six.

106
00:03:50,707 --> 00:03:52,814
The rest came from new members signing

107
00:03:52,814 --> 00:03:55,156
up and from members upgrading to the

108
00:03:55,156 --> 00:03:56,926
more expensive card.

109
00:03:57,183 --> 00:03:59,713
And people keep paying.

110
00:04:00,003 --> 00:04:02,124
Costco reports a renewal rate of

111
00:04:02,124 --> 00:04:04,244
ninety-two point three percent in the

112
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:06,753
United States and Canada.

113
00:04:07,003 --> 00:04:08,932
More than nine in ten members sign

114
00:04:08,932 --> 00:04:09,872
up again,

115
00:04:09,872 --> 00:04:11,054
year after year,

116
00:04:11,054 --> 00:04:13,583
even after a price increase.

117
00:04:13,773 --> 00:04:14,487
Worldwide,

118
00:04:14,487 --> 00:04:17,195
including the newer countries where Costco

119
00:04:17,195 --> 00:04:18,401
is still growing,

120
00:04:18,401 --> 00:04:20,371
the rate is eighty-nine point eight

121
00:04:20,371 --> 00:04:21,463
percent.

122
00:04:21,603 --> 00:04:23,872
For a subscription that people pay for

123
00:04:23,872 --> 00:04:24,756
in advance,

124
00:04:24,756 --> 00:04:27,171
those are remarkable numbers.

125
00:04:27,417 --> 00:04:30,361
Multiply millions of members by a yearly

126
00:04:30,361 --> 00:04:30,827
fee,

127
00:04:30,827 --> 00:04:32,790
and you get five point nine billion

128
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:35,107
dollars in a single year.

129
00:04:35,407 --> 00:04:36,857
Fees have their own costs,

130
00:04:36,857 --> 00:04:39,177
so they are not pure profit.

131
00:04:39,347 --> 00:04:41,741
But they arrive almost regardless of what

132
00:04:41,741 --> 00:04:42,590
members buy,

133
00:04:42,590 --> 00:04:44,766
and they equal about half of Costco's

134
00:04:44,766 --> 00:04:46,672
operating profit.

135
00:04:46,917 --> 00:04:48,997
Let me draw the two halves of

136
00:04:48,997 --> 00:04:50,117
Costco.

137
00:04:50,507 --> 00:04:51,498
On one side,

138
00:04:51,498 --> 00:04:52,235
the store,

139
00:04:52,235 --> 00:04:54,867
selling goods at a thin margin.

140
00:04:54,967 --> 00:04:56,351
On the other side,

141
00:04:56,351 --> 00:04:57,031
the club,

142
00:04:57,031 --> 00:04:59,507
collecting fees every year.

143
00:04:59,637 --> 00:05:01,867
The low prices bring members in.

144
00:05:01,867 --> 00:05:04,257
The fees pay for the low prices.

145
00:05:04,257 --> 00:05:06,654
And because members have already paid,

146
00:05:06,654 --> 00:05:08,413
they keep coming back to get their

147
00:05:08,413 --> 00:05:09,673
money's worth.

148
00:05:09,917 --> 00:05:12,528
But low prices do not come from

149
00:05:12,528 --> 00:05:14,107
fees alone.

150
00:05:14,167 --> 00:05:16,809
They also come from what Costco refuses

151
00:05:16,809 --> 00:05:17,890
to sell.

152
00:05:23,550 --> 00:05:25,857
Costco says it carries fewer than four

153
00:05:25,857 --> 00:05:28,371
thousand active products per warehouse,

154
00:05:28,371 --> 00:05:30,678
significantly less than other big

155
00:05:30,678 --> 00:05:31,970
retailers.

156
00:05:31,990 --> 00:05:34,633
Instead of twenty brands of ketchup,

157
00:05:34,633 --> 00:05:35,942
you might find two,

158
00:05:35,942 --> 00:05:37,877
often in giant packs.

159
00:05:38,117 --> 00:05:41,073
Walk into any Costco and the design

160
00:05:41,073 --> 00:05:43,017
tells the same story.

161
00:05:43,167 --> 00:05:44,330
Concrete floors,

162
00:05:44,330 --> 00:05:45,791
tall steel shelves,

163
00:05:45,791 --> 00:05:48,657
goods still on their shipping pallets.

164
00:05:48,657 --> 00:05:50,124
No fancy displays,

165
00:05:50,124 --> 00:05:51,967
no decoration.

166
00:05:52,147 --> 00:05:54,482
Every dollar not spent on the store

167
00:05:54,482 --> 00:05:56,350
is a dollar that can come off

168
00:05:56,350 --> 00:05:57,417
the price.

169
00:05:57,650 --> 00:05:59,340
That short list is the trick.

170
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:01,364
With nine hundred and thirty-nine

171
00:06:01,364 --> 00:06:03,615
warehouses around the world,

172
00:06:03,615 --> 00:06:06,145
every product on the shelf sells in

173
00:06:06,145 --> 00:06:08,010
enormous volume.

174
00:06:08,070 --> 00:06:11,087
And enormous volume gives Costco enormous

175
00:06:11,087 --> 00:06:13,640
bargaining power with the companies that

176
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:14,936
make the goods.

177
00:06:15,183 --> 00:06:17,195
Then there is Costco's own brand,

178
00:06:17,195 --> 00:06:18,698
Kirkland Signature,

179
00:06:18,698 --> 00:06:21,499
whose trademark turned thirty in twenty

180
00:06:21,499 --> 00:06:22,863
twenty-five.

181
00:06:23,083 --> 00:06:25,045
In its latest annual letter,

182
00:06:25,045 --> 00:06:27,029
Costco says Kirkland brings in about

183
00:06:27,029 --> 00:06:29,652
ninety billion dollars a year worldwide,

184
00:06:29,652 --> 00:06:32,297
and typically saves members fifteen to

185
00:06:32,297 --> 00:06:34,943
twenty percent compared with the national

186
00:06:34,943 --> 00:06:35,763
brand.

187
00:06:35,883 --> 00:06:38,387
And according to its annual report,

188
00:06:38,387 --> 00:06:40,685
these products generally earn Costco

189
00:06:40,685 --> 00:06:42,204
higher margins.

190
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:44,650
Let me draw the difference.

191
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:46,873
Here is a typical supermarket,

192
00:06:46,873 --> 00:06:48,360
with a long list of products,

193
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:50,090
each selling a little.

194
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:51,919
Here is Costco,

195
00:06:51,919 --> 00:06:53,538
with a short list,

196
00:06:53,538 --> 00:06:55,590
each selling a lot.

197
00:06:55,590 --> 00:06:56,209
Same shoppers,

198
00:06:56,209 --> 00:06:57,955
but the money is concentrated on far

199
00:06:57,955 --> 00:06:59,190
fewer items.

200
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:02,002
That is what lets Costco squeeze the

201
00:07:02,002 --> 00:07:03,824
price on each one.

202
00:07:04,083 --> 00:07:06,472
And the speed at which those products

203
00:07:06,472 --> 00:07:09,159
sell unlocks the most hidden part of

204
00:07:09,159 --> 00:07:10,953
the business.

205
00:07:11,463 --> 00:07:15,280
Costco often gets paid before it pays.

206
00:07:20,917 --> 00:07:22,498
This is Costco,

207
00:07:22,498 --> 00:07:25,357
in its own annual report.

208
00:07:25,717 --> 00:07:28,261
We often sell inventory before we are

209
00:07:28,261 --> 00:07:29,935
required to pay for it,

210
00:07:29,935 --> 00:07:32,479
even while taking advantage of early

211
00:07:32,479 --> 00:07:33,867
payment discounts.

212
00:07:33,867 --> 00:07:35,247
Read that again.

213
00:07:35,247 --> 00:07:37,508
Costco can pay its suppliers early,

214
00:07:37,508 --> 00:07:39,541
collect a discount for doing it,

215
00:07:39,541 --> 00:07:41,139
and still have sold the goods to

216
00:07:41,139 --> 00:07:44,252
you before the money leaves its account.

217
00:07:44,517 --> 00:07:46,717
Look at the balance sheet.

218
00:07:46,717 --> 00:07:49,459
At the end of August twenty twenty-six,

219
00:07:49,459 --> 00:07:51,762
Costco held about nineteen point three

220
00:07:51,762 --> 00:07:53,605
billion dollars of goods in its

221
00:07:53,605 --> 00:07:55,127
warehouses.

222
00:07:55,207 --> 00:07:56,593
At the same moment,

223
00:07:56,593 --> 00:07:59,177
it owed its suppliers twenty-two point six

224
00:07:59,177 --> 00:08:00,717
billion dollars.

225
00:08:00,717 --> 00:08:02,304
It owed more than the value of

226
00:08:02,304 --> 00:08:04,491
everything on its shelves.

227
00:08:04,750 --> 00:08:05,790
How is that possible?

228
00:08:05,790 --> 00:08:07,020
Speed.

229
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:09,283
By our calculation,

230
00:08:09,283 --> 00:08:12,187
Costco's entire inventory sells through

231
00:08:12,187 --> 00:08:15,110
about fourteen times a year.

232
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:17,486
A typical item sits in the warehouse

233
00:08:17,486 --> 00:08:19,030
for less than a month.

234
00:08:19,330 --> 00:08:21,636
If the supplier gives Costco about a

235
00:08:21,636 --> 00:08:22,454
month to pay,

236
00:08:22,454 --> 00:08:24,970
the cash from the customer often arrives

237
00:08:24,970 --> 00:08:25,780
first.

238
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:27,182
A slower store,

239
00:08:27,182 --> 00:08:28,786
where goods sit on the shelf for

240
00:08:28,786 --> 00:08:29,221
months,

241
00:08:29,221 --> 00:08:31,512
would have to find that money somewhere

242
00:08:31,512 --> 00:08:32,341
else.

243
00:08:32,583 --> 00:08:34,863
Let me draw the cash cycle.

244
00:08:34,923 --> 00:08:35,813
Day zero,

245
00:08:35,813 --> 00:08:37,543
a pallet arrives.

246
00:08:37,543 --> 00:08:38,851
A few weeks later,

247
00:08:38,851 --> 00:08:40,159
members have bought it,

248
00:08:40,159 --> 00:08:42,533
and the cash is in Costco's bank.

249
00:08:42,643 --> 00:08:44,581
Only later does the bill to the

250
00:08:44,581 --> 00:08:46,043
supplier come due.

251
00:08:46,093 --> 00:08:47,021
In between,

252
00:08:47,021 --> 00:08:49,399
Costco holds billions of dollars that are

253
00:08:49,399 --> 00:08:51,278
really its suppliers' money,

254
00:08:51,278 --> 00:08:53,180
and it can use that money to

255
00:08:53,180 --> 00:08:54,969
run the business.

256
00:08:55,217 --> 00:08:58,957
One more piece ties everything together.

257
00:08:59,007 --> 00:09:01,502
The members who spend the most.

258
00:09:07,150 --> 00:09:09,864
Executive members pay double the basic

259
00:09:09,864 --> 00:09:10,690
fee.

260
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:11,653
In return,

261
00:09:11,653 --> 00:09:13,669
they get back about two percent of

262
00:09:13,669 --> 00:09:14,542
what they spend,

263
00:09:14,542 --> 00:09:16,710
up to an annual limit.

264
00:09:16,990 --> 00:09:19,426
Costco had forty-two point three million

265
00:09:19,426 --> 00:09:21,000
of them last year.

266
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:23,734
And they accounted for about seventy-five

267
00:09:23,734 --> 00:09:26,220
percent of Costco's sales worldwide.

268
00:09:26,530 --> 00:09:27,679
Two years earlier,

269
00:09:27,679 --> 00:09:30,390
there were thirty-five point four million.

270
00:09:30,630 --> 00:09:31,762
Every year,

271
00:09:31,762 --> 00:09:33,840
more members choose to pay the higher

272
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:34,671
fee.

273
00:09:34,917 --> 00:09:37,807
Think about what that reward does.

274
00:09:37,877 --> 00:09:39,786
The more you spend at Costco,

275
00:09:39,786 --> 00:09:41,212
the more you get back,

276
00:09:41,212 --> 00:09:42,904
so the more you want to buy

277
00:09:42,904 --> 00:09:45,437
there instead of somewhere else.

278
00:09:45,587 --> 00:09:47,837
The fee makes you come back.

279
00:09:47,887 --> 00:09:50,091
The reward makes you spend more when

280
00:09:50,091 --> 00:09:51,180
you do.

281
00:09:51,417 --> 00:09:54,567
Let me put the whole machine together.

282
00:09:54,587 --> 00:09:57,087
Low prices bring members in.

283
00:09:57,137 --> 00:09:58,291
Members pay fees,

284
00:09:58,291 --> 00:10:01,247
which equal about half of the profit.

285
00:10:01,367 --> 00:10:03,866
A short list of products sells in

286
00:10:03,866 --> 00:10:05,085
huge volume,

287
00:10:05,085 --> 00:10:07,247
which keeps prices low.

288
00:10:07,407 --> 00:10:10,057
Fast sales let Costco use its suppliers'

289
00:10:10,057 --> 00:10:11,187
money.

290
00:10:11,307 --> 00:10:13,809
And the biggest spenders are rewarded for

291
00:10:13,809 --> 00:10:15,547
spending even more.

292
00:10:15,587 --> 00:10:18,273
Each part feeds the next.

293
00:10:18,517 --> 00:10:22,109
So how does Costco make money selling

294
00:10:22,109 --> 00:10:23,427
things cheap?

295
00:10:23,577 --> 00:10:25,354
It does not really try to profit

296
00:10:25,354 --> 00:10:26,547
on the goods.

297
00:10:26,627 --> 00:10:28,677
It sells access.

298
00:10:28,717 --> 00:10:30,509
The cheap prices are the product that

299
00:10:30,509 --> 00:10:32,082
keeps millions of people paying,

300
00:10:32,082 --> 00:10:33,455
every single year,

301
00:10:33,455 --> 00:10:35,407
for the privilege of shopping there.

302
00:10:35,407 --> 00:10:36,877
The store is the bait.

303
00:10:37,027 --> 00:10:38,757
The membership is the business.

304
00:10:38,797 --> 00:10:40,301
And for members,

305
00:10:40,301 --> 00:10:42,755
the deal works only as long as

306
00:10:42,755 --> 00:10:46,117
the savings are bigger than the fee.

307
00:10:46,317 --> 00:10:48,384
That is the promise Costco has to

308
00:10:48,384 --> 00:10:48,820
keep,

309
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:50,405
every single year,

310
00:10:50,405 --> 00:10:51,924
to stay on top.

311
00:10:52,183 --> 00:10:54,558
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

312
00:10:54,558 --> 00:10:57,390
we follow the money behind another hidden

313
00:10:57,390 --> 00:10:59,733
link of the world economy.

314
00:10:59,823 --> 00:11:01,898
This is The Hidden Economics.
